y</em>-intercept of the graph of <math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane. The slope of the graph of <math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> can be found using the formula <math alttext=\"m equals StartFraction y 2 minus y 1 Over x 2 minus x 1 EndFraction\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>, where <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x 1 comma y 1 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x 2 comma y 2 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> are any two points that the graph passes through. If <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis 0 right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></math>, it follows that the graph of <math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> passes through the point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 17 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. If <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis 1 right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></math>, it follows that the graph of <math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> passes through the point <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 17 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 17 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 17 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> for <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x 1 comma y 1 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math alttext=\"left parenthesis x 2 comma y 2 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>, respectively, in the formula <math alttext=\"m equals StartFraction y 2 minus y 1 Over x 2 minus x 1 EndFraction\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"m equals StartFraction 17 minus 17 Over 1 minus 0 EndFraction\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"m equals StartFraction 0 Over 1 EndFraction\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"m equals 0\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Since the graph of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> passes through <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 17 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>, it follows that <math alttext=\"b equals 17\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"m\"><mi>m</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math> in the equation <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals m x plus b\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></math> yields <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals 0 x plus 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>17</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></math>. Thus, the equation that defines <math alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis x right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For the linear function <math alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis 0 right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mn>0</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"f left parenthesis 1 right parenthesis equals 17\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>17</mn></mrow></math>. Which equation defines <math alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi>\n</math>?</p>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1730147357390,"updateDate":1730147357390}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357390, 1730147357390),
    ($SATQ$feb78194$SATQ$, $SATQ$4b647461-1e29-465f-a480-e7b4dcecc200$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear equations in one variable$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>A museum rents tablets to visitors. The museum earns revenue of <math alttext="dollar sign 14"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn>
</math> for each tablet rented for the day. On Wednesday, the museum earned <math alttext="dollar sign 406"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn>
</math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math alttext="dollar sign 112"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn>
</math>. How many tablets did the museum rent on Wednesday? <math alttext="left parenthesis profit equals total revenue minus total expenses right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>&#8722;</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></mrow></mfenced></math></p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["37"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["37"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="37"><mn>37</mn></math>. It's given that the museum earns revenue of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="dollar sign 14"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn></math> for each tablet rented for the day. It's also given that on Wednesday, the museum earned <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="dollar sign 406"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn></math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="dollar sign 112"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn></math>. Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the number of tablets the museum rented on Wednesday. It follows that the total revenue can be represented by the expression <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="14 x"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Because&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="profit equals total revenue minus total expenses"><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>-</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></math>, the equation <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="406 equals 14 x minus 112"><mn>406</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;represents this situation. Adding <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="112"><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="14 x equals 518"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>518</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="14"><mn>14</mn></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 37"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></math>. Therefore, the museum rented <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="37"><mn>37</mn></math> tablets on Wednesday.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"feb78194","external_id":"4b647461-1e29-465f-a480-e7b4dcecc200","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"a034c170-ab56-4812-9d1c-305dbde10dcb","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.A.","skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>A museum rents tablets to visitors. The museum earns revenue of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 14\"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn>\n</math> for each tablet rented for the day. On Wednesday, the museum earned <math alttext=\"dollar sign 406\"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn>\n</math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 112\"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn>\n</math>. How many tablets did the museum rent on Wednesday? <math alttext=\"left parenthesis profit equals total revenue minus total expenses right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>&#8722;</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></mrow></mfenced></math></p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["37"],"correct_answer":["37"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn></math>. It's given that the museum earns revenue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 14\"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn></math> for each tablet rented for the day. It's also given that on Wednesday, the museum earned <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 406\"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn></math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 112\"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the number of tablets the museum rented on Wednesday. It follows that the total revenue can be represented by the expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Because&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"profit equals total revenue minus total expenses\"><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>-</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></math>, the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"406 equals 14 x minus 112\"><mn>406</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;represents this situation. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"112\"><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x equals 518\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>518</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 37\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></math>. Therefore, the museum rented <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn></math> tablets on Wednesday.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017718,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"feb78194","skill_cd":"H.A.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Linear equations in one variable","createDate":1755117017718,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"","external_id":"4b647461-1e29-465f-a480-e7b4dcecc200","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"a034c170-ab56-4812-9d1c-305dbde10dcb","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p>A museum rents tablets to visitors. The museum earns revenue of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 14\"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn>\n</math> for each tablet rented for the day. On Wednesday, the museum earned <math alttext=\"dollar sign 406\"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn>\n</math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 112\"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn>\n</math>. How many tablets did the museum rent on Wednesday? <math alttext=\"left parenthesis profit equals total revenue minus total expenses right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>&#8722;</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></mrow></mfenced></math></p>","keys":["37"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn></math>. It's given that the museum earns revenue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 14\"><mo>$</mo><mn>14</mn></math> for each tablet rented for the day. It's also given that on Wednesday, the museum earned <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 406\"><mo>$</mo><mn>406</mn></math> in profit from renting tablets after paying daily expenses of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"dollar sign 112\"><mo>$</mo><mn>112</mn></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the number of tablets the museum rented on Wednesday. It follows that the total revenue can be represented by the expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Because&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"profit equals total revenue minus total expenses\"><mtext>profit</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>revenue</mtext><mo>-</mo><mtext>total</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>expenses</mtext></math>, the equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"406 equals 14 x minus 112\"><mn>406</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;represents this situation. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"112\"><mn>112</mn></math>&nbsp;to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 x equals 518\"><mn>14</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>518</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 37\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></math>. Therefore, the museum rented <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"37\"><mn>37</mn></math> tablets on Wednesday.</p>","externalid":"4b647461-1e29-465f-a480-e7b4dcecc200","correct_answer":["37"]},"createDate":1755117017718,"updateDate":1755117017718}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017718, 1755117017718),
    ($SATQ$ff501705$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c09d547-9da9-4b9a-a436-ddc8c0c70764$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0801cf6e-2505-4448-816f-78bbe9068136$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="three halves y minus one fourth x equals two thirds minus three halves y"><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>-</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>y</mi></math></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="one half x plus three halves equals p y plus nine halves"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">In the given system of equations, <math alttext="p"><mi>p</mi>
</math> is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of <math alttext="p"><mi>p</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math alttext="6"><mn>6</mn>
</math>. A system of two linear equations in two variables, <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math>, has no solution if the lines represented by the equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane are parallel and distinct. Lines represented by equations in standard form, <math alttext="upper A x plus upper B y equals upper C"><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></math> and <math alttext="upper D x plus upper E y equals upper F"><mi>D</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></math>, are parallel if the coefficients for <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> in one equation are proportional to the corresponding coefficients in the other equation, meaning <math alttext="StartFraction upper D Over upper A EndFraction equals StartFraction upper E Over upper B EndFraction"><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac></math>;&nbsp;and the lines are distinct if the constants are not proportional, meaning <math alttext="StartFraction upper F Over upper C EndFraction"><mfrac><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></mfrac></math> is not equal to&nbsp;<math alttext="StartFraction upper D Over upper A EndFraction"><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math> or <math alttext="StartFraction upper E Over upper B EndFraction"><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac></math>. The first equation in the given system is <math alttext="three halves y minus one fourth x equals two thirds minus three halves y"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext="12"><mn>12</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="18 y minus 3 x equals 8 minus 18 y"><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi></math>. Adding <math alttext="18 y"><mrow>
	<mn>18</mn>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math> to each side of this equation yields <math alttext="36 y minus 3 x equals 8"><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math>, or <math alttext="minus 3 x plus 36 y equals 8"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math>. The second equation in the given system is <math alttext="one half x plus three halves equals p y plus nine halves"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext="2"><mn>2</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="x plus 3 equals 2 p y plus 9"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="2 p y"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>p</mi>
	<mi>y</mi>
</mrow>
</math> from each side of this equation yields <math alttext="x plus 3 minus 2 p y equals 9"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math> from each side of this equation yields <math alttext="x minus 2 p y equals 6"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. Therefore, the two equations in the given system, written in standard form, are&nbsp;<math alttext="minus 3 x plus 36 y equals 8"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math> and<math alttext="x minus 2 p y equals 6"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. As previously stated, if this system has no solution, the lines represented by the equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane are parallel and distinct, meaning the proportion&nbsp;<math alttext="StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction equals StartFraction minus 2 p Over 36 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow><mn>36</mn></mfrac></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="negative one third equals minus StartFraction p Over 18 EndFraction"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>18</mn></mfrac></math>, is true and the proportion <math alttext="six eighths equals StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is not true. The proportion <math alttext="six eighths equals StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is not true. Multiplying each side of the true proportion, <math alttext="negative one third equals minus StartFraction p Over 18 EndFraction"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>18</mn></mfrac></math>,&nbsp; by <math alttext="negative 18"><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="6 equals p"><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Therefore, if the system has no solution, then the value of <math alttext="p"><mi>p</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="6"><mn>6</mn>
</math>.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"ff501705","external_id":"5c09d547-9da9-4b9a-a436-ddc8c0c70764","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"0801cf6e-2505-4448-816f-78bbe9068136","uId":"0752e3f1-5acc-45cf-883e-5a93989a9531","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.D.","skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"three halves y minus one fourth x equals two thirds minus three halves y\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>-</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>y</mi></math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"one half x plus three halves equals p y plus nine halves\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the given system of equations, <math alttext=\"p\"><mi>p</mi>\n</math> is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of <math alttext=\"p\"><mi>p</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["6"],"correct_answer":["6"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>. A system of two linear equations in two variables, <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math>, has no solution if the lines represented by the equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane are parallel and distinct. Lines represented by equations in standard form, <math alttext=\"upper A x plus upper B y equals upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"upper D x plus upper E y equals upper F\"><mi>D</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></math>, are parallel if the coefficients for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> in one equation are proportional to the corresponding coefficients in the other equation, meaning <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D Over upper A EndFraction equals StartFraction upper E Over upper B EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac></math>;&nbsp;and the lines are distinct if the constants are not proportional, meaning <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper F Over upper C EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></mfrac></math> is not equal to&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D Over upper A EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math> or <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper E Over upper B EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac></math>. The first equation in the given system is <math alttext=\"three halves y minus one fourth x equals two thirds minus three halves y\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>y</mi></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"18 y minus 3 x equals 8 minus 18 y\"><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi></math>. Adding <math alttext=\"18 y\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> to each side of this equation yields <math alttext=\"36 y minus 3 x equals 8\"><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"minus 3 x plus 36 y equals 8\"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math>. The second equation in the given system is <math alttext=\"one half x plus three halves equals p y plus nine halves\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x plus 3 equals 2 p y plus 9\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext=\"2 p y\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>p</mi>\n\t<mi>y</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> from each side of this equation yields <math alttext=\"x plus 3 minus 2 p y equals 9\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math> from each side of this equation yields <math alttext=\"x minus 2 p y equals 6\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. Therefore, the two equations in the given system, written in standard form, are&nbsp;<math alttext=\"minus 3 x plus 36 y equals 8\"><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math> and<math alttext=\"x minus 2 p y equals 6\"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>. As previously stated, if this system has no solution, the lines represented by the equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane are parallel and distinct, meaning the proportion&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction equals StartFraction minus 2 p Over 36 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow><mn>36</mn></mfrac></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"negative one third equals minus StartFraction p Over 18 EndFraction\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>18</mn></mfrac></math>, is true and the proportion <math alttext=\"six eighths equals StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is not true. The proportion <math alttext=\"six eighths equals StartFraction 1 Over negative 3 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is not true. Multiplying each side of the true proportion, <math alttext=\"negative one third equals minus StartFraction p Over 18 EndFraction\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>18</mn></mfrac></math>,&nbsp; by <math alttext=\"negative 18\"><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"6 equals p\"><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Therefore, if the system has no solution, then the value of <math alttext=\"p\"><mi>p</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959817,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"ff501705","skill_cd":"H.D.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Systems of two linear equations in two variables","createDate":1691007959817,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":null,"external_id":"5c09d547-9da9-4b9a-a436-ddc8c0c70764","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"0752e3f1-5acc-45cf-883e-5a93989a9531","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["6"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>. A system of two linear equations in two variables, <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math>, has no solution if the lines represented by the equations in the <em>xy</em>-plane are parallel and distinct. Lines represented 